another problematic thing with languages is how unusable they are.
very often programming language usage is heavily dependent on specific IDE (look java), or require lsp (because code doesn't communicate nor data flows well or naming in the language is highly ambigous)

language should be greppable. this might seem like it introduces another tooling dependency but in reality search over stream of characters is one of the most basic operations provided by all sorts of different tools.

language should be editor-independent.
the compiler should be the user interface of the program - if an error is detected during compile time the error message should clear display
1) whats wrong
2) where
3) why?
4) how to fix it
5) potentially guess the intention of the user and where the point of misunderstanding might come from (if it was not a typo), that's why the syntax cannot mean 20 different things

compile time should be so fast that the developer wants to run it all the time, could even be a simple bash loop that recompiles the whole program (no incremental builds or magical caching under the hood). same rule as any sane person familiar with testing, can't take too long.
generally compile time <=1s is good for complex programs.

one way to make sure language is editor independent and searchable would be to drop any kind of 'auto' keywords or python-like typeless declarations.
this unfortunately adds noise for person that knows what they're doing but adds value to person that will have to read it to understand how data flows.

one thing could be to use the hungarian notation but it's ugly as hell.

and also the primary thing is not the typechecking (this is what compiler does)
but giving the developer ability to understand program, find definitions wihout lsp, understand implementation better, understand how to use a thing better e.g. function params etc.

